--- Covariate balance ---
   Variable           Coef(conv)  p(robust)       h*
   -------------------------------------------------- 
   female                -0.0234     0.6057    0.224
   firstgen              -0.0154     0.7628    0.224
   urm                   -0.0210     0.7547    0.247
   act_score             -0.0197     0.7824    0.300
   income_cat             0.2517     0.0422    0.132
   -------------------------------------------------- 
   None should be statistically significant under a valid RD.
   Failing to detect a jump is not the same as establishing that these
   characteristics are identical across the threshold: the tests are
   local and therefore not highly powered.
   If ONE covariate jumps, do not abandon the design on that alone:
   with five tests at the .05 level, the chance that at least one
   rejects by chance is about 23 percent. Check three things instead --
   whether the jump survives other bandwidths and polynomial orders,
   whether its h* is far narrower than the others (a narrow window is a
   small effective sample, not evidence of sorting), and whether adding
   the covariate moves the treatment estimate. A jump that vanishes
   under respecification and leaves the estimate unchanged is noise;
   one that persists and shifts the estimate is a threat to the design.

